            <h3>Honolulu Airport Terminals</h3>
            <p>HNL operates multiple terminals in a somewhat sprawling configuration that has seen recent modernization efforts. </p>
            <h4>Terminal 1 (Interisland Terminal) </h4>
            <p>Primarily serves Hawaiian Airlines' extensive inter-island network. </p>
            <h5>Facilities: </h5>
            <ul>
                <li>Inter-island check-in and security</li>
                <li>Hawaiian Airlines Premier Club</li>
                <li>Basic retail and dining</li>
            </ul>
            <h4>Terminal 2 (Overseas Terminal) </h4>
            <p>Handles all mainland and international flights. </p>
            <h5>Facilities: </h5>
            <ul>
                <li>Mainland and international check-in</li>
                <li>Agricultural inspection for arriving passengers (mandatory for all Hawaii arrivals)</li>
                <li>Baggage claim and ground transportation</li>
                <li>Limited but improving dining and retail options</li>
            </ul>
            <h4>Terminal 3 (Commuter Terminal) </h4>
            <p>Serves smaller commuter and regional operators. </p>
            <h3>Agricultural Inspection at Honolulu Airport</h3>
            <p>All passengers arriving in Hawaii must clear agricultural inspection, a unique requirement designed to protect the islands' fragile ecosystem from invasive species. </p>
            <h4>Requirements: </h4>
            <ul>
                <li>Declare all plants, animals, and food products</li>
                <li>Expect baggage screening by agricultural dogs and X-ray machines</li>
                <li>Prohibited items include fresh fruits, vegetables, and certain flowers</li>
                <li>Prepared foods and sealed snacks are generally permitted</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>Transportation from Honolulu Airport to Waikiki and Beyond</h3>
            <h4>SpeediShuttle and Shared Rides</h4>
            <p>The most popular option for tourists, offering door-to-door service to Waikiki hotels. </p>
            <h5>Features: </h5>
            <ul>
                <li>Cost approximately $15-$18 per person shared</li>
                <li>Private options available for families</li>
                <li>Journey time: 30-45 minutes depending on hotel location</li>
            </ul>
            <h4>Taxi</h4>
            <p>Fixed-rate service to Waikiki and other destinations. </p>
            <h5>Typical fares: </h5>
            <ul>
                <li>To Waikiki: Approximately $40-$50 including baggage fees</li>
                <li>To Ko Olina: Approximately $80-$100</li>
                <li>Always use official taxi stands</li>
            </ul>
            <h4>Rideshare</h4>
            <p>Uber and Lyft operate from designated pickup zones at HNL. </p>
            <h4>TheBus</h4>
            <p>Public bus service offering the most economical option at approximately $3.00, though with limited luggage capacity and longer journey times. </p>
            <h4>Rental Cars</h4>
            <p>All major agencies maintain facilities at HNL. Note that rental car availability has been constrained in recent years, and reservations are essential. </p>
